    The file states one final theorem, `Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime_modEq_one`: for every natural number k with k ≠ 0, the set of primes p with p ≡ 1 (mod k) is infinite. [C:statement/Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime_modEq_one] The module note says the file proves that for any positive k there are infinitely many primes p with p ≡ 1 (mod k), which is the statement in other words. [N:aim/primes-congruent-to-one]

    The argument has three stages. The first is a reduction. An infinite set of natural numbers has a member beyond every bound, so it is enough to find, for each n, one prime larger than n that is 1 modulo k. The obvious way would be to list the primes that leave remainder 1 and show the list never ends, and nothing lists them. [C:part/what-must-be-found]

    The main stage is the one spine lemma, `Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one`: for every natural number n and every natural number k with k ≠ 0 there is a prime p with n < p and p ≡ 1 (mod k). Without it nothing produces a prime of the right kind, and it turns infinitely many into one beyond any n. [C:lemma/Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one, C:part/a-number-built-to-have-the-right-factor] When k is 1 every prime is 1 modulo k, so the file takes any prime beyond n; it leans on Euclid's theorem, in the form that past any n there is a prime. [C:step/the-case-k-is-one, C:reliance/infinitely-many-primes] For k at least 2 the proof fixes the point b = k · n! and evaluates the k-th cyclotomic polynomial there. [C:constant/the-point] A library bound on cyclotomic values, used for the polynomial of index larger than 1, makes the value larger than 1 in absolute value, so it has a least prime factor p. [C:step/the-value-exceeds-one, C:reliance/a-cyclotomic-value-is-large] Because p divides the value, b is a root of the k-th cyclotomic polynomial modulo p, and a root of a cyclotomic polynomial modulo p is prime to p, so p does not divide b. [C:step/b-is-a-root-modulo-p, C:reliance/a-root-is-prime-to-p] If p were at most n it would divide n!, hence b, which it does not; so p is larger than n. [C:step/p-exceeds-n] Last, the order of b modulo p divides p − 1, which is Fermat's little theorem in the form that the order divides p − 1, and the order equals k, because where k is not zero a root of the k-th cyclotomic polynomial is a primitive k-th root of unity. So k divides p − 1 and p ≡ 1 (mod k). [C:step/the-order-of-b-is-k, C:reliance/the-order-divides-p-minus-one, C:reliance/a-root-has-order-k]

    The close only rephrases. `Nat.frequently_atTop_modEq_one` restates the spine lemma in the language of filters: such primes occur beyond every bound. We read it as bookkeeping, though it sits one step from the final theorem. With one such prime beyond every n, the primes that are 1 modulo k occur beyond every bound, and a set of natural numbers with members beyond every bound is infinite. [C:lemma/Nat.frequently_atTop_modEq_one, C:part/from-one-to-many]

    What the proof delivers is more than the statement says. The statement asserts that the set is infinite; the construction names the prime. For every k ≥ 2 and every n, the least prime factor p of the value of the k-th cyclotomic polynomial at k · n! satisfies p > n and p ≡ 1 (mod k). The file itself takes every step to this. The one thing the proof chooses is the point the polynomial is evaluated at: for a point at least 2 that is a multiple of k, it gives a prime that is 1 modulo k and does not divide the point, and that prime exceeds n when n! divides the point. In the case k = 1 every prime qualifies, and the proof delivers a prime beyond n by the known infinitude of primes, which equals what is plainly true there. [P:yield/what-the-proof-delivers] For k = 3 and n = 2 the point is 3 · 2! = 6, the third cyclotomic polynomial is x² + x + 1, and at 6 it gives 43, whose least prime factor is 43, larger than 2 and equal to 3 · 14 + 1. [P:case/k-three-n-two]

    Two readings say why this works. A prime that divides a value of the k-th cyclotomic polynomial and does not divide the point is 1 modulo k, so the proof only has to make such a prime large. Every number from 1 to n divides n!, so a prime that does not divide k · n! is larger than n. [P:reading/the-polynomial-does-the-work, P:reading/the-factorial-pushes-the-prime-past-n] The file was read as text, and names were matched by spelling, so a link between two declarations may be missing. [P:assumption/read-as-text]
